test_cross_as — user-process → kernel-server IPC validation
============================================================

Looks up the kernel-thread echo server registered under the port
name "echo" (see board/virt/hello.lsysl DEMO_MODE=4, kentry_a),
sends two bytes, echoes each reply back to the UART via putc,
then exits cleanly. Exercises the cross-address-space ipc_deliver
/ vm_copy_cross path end-to-end.

Startup race: kentry_a may not have finished port_register when we
run, so retry the lookup a few times (yielding between tries) until
the port shows up. This is how real user programs will deal with
server startup ordering too.

    module oskit.bin.test_cross_as

    import oskit.ulib.{putc, exit, port_lookup, ipc_send, yield}

    main(argv: []string)
        var port = -1
        var attempts = 0
        while port < 0
            if attempts >= 16
                putc('X')
                putc('\n')
                exit()
            yield()
            port = port_lookup("echo")
            attempts += 1

        var send_buf: [1]byte
        var reply_buf: [1]byte

        send_buf[0] = byte('O')
        ipc_send(port, *byte(send_buf), 1,
                 *byte(reply_buf), 1)
        putc(int(reply_buf[0]))

        send_buf[0] = byte('K')
        ipc_send(port, *byte(send_buf), 1,
                 *byte(reply_buf), 1)
        putc(int(reply_buf[0]))

        putc('\n')
        exit()
